PROJECT GOAL: We aim to verify if the user needs to set up a build environment to resolve the issue:

1. Does not need build environment
   (The solutions/answers can be validated without build environment, e.g., it's a conceptual issue, documentation issue, or can be verified with simple code analysis)

2. Can be dockerized without any issue
   (The solutions must require a build environment to be verified)

3. Requires build environment but hard to be dockerized or cannot be reproduced now
   (The solution must require a build environment, but it is difficult to containerize for reasons such as: hardware dependencies, network dependencies, external API access with authentication, timing-dependent behaviors, requiring MacOS, multiple users interacting simultaneously, licensed software, etc.)

IMPORTANT GUIDELINES:
- Choose category 1 whenever possible. If the LLM can analyze the issue and verify the solution without needing to build or run the code, always select category 1.
- Only classify an issue as requiring a build environment (categories 2 or 3) when it's ABSOLUTELY NECESSARY to actually build and run the code to verify if the solution works.

GitHub Issue:
Post Date: 2012-04-06T21:46:51Z
Current Date: April 4 2025
Title: Can't change color of LineBasicMaterial or MeshBasicMaterial
Description: (providing example for LineBasicMaterial, but it's the same for MeshBasicMaterial)

I'd like to randomly change the color of some lines every frame, without having to re-create the lines, or creating/associating a new material for them. So I thought to just change the "color" property of their material...

I tried a few tricks ( i.e. setting the followings to true: `geometry.dynamic geometry.dirtyColors geometry.__dirtyVertices geometry.__dirtyNormals` ) , but I still can't change the color of a line dynamically.

See minimal code below.

The line is drawn with a random color for one frame, and then:
- using webgl, it's drawn black. I.e. the color created at construction "sticks" to the material, and subsequent changes just turn the line black...
- using the canvas renderer, the changes are disregarded and only the first color sticks.

``` javascript
    function animate() {
        requestAnimationFrame( animate );
        render();
    }

   var material;

   function render() {

     // setting this to false creates a material
     // with random color each frame
     // (which works fine, just to show that
     // the code has no major flaws)
     var dynamicMaterialColor = true;

     if (material === undefined || ! dynamicMaterialColor ) {
       material = new THREE.LineBasicMaterial({
         color: Math.floor(Math.random() * 16777216),
       });
     }
     // if the material has already been created,
     // re-use it and just change the color.
     else {
       // Attempting to change the color of the material.
       // Commenting this line below shows that
       // the first color *does* stick to the material.
       // It's subsequent changes that turn the line
       // black.
       material.color = Math.floor(Math.random() * 16777216);
     }

    // just creates the geometry of the line. 
    var geometry = new THREE.Geometry();
     geometry.vertices.push(new THREE.Vertex(new THREE.Vector3(-20, 0, 0)));
     geometry.vertices.push(new THREE.Vertex(new THREE.Vector3(20, 0, 0)));

    // creates the line object and add to scene
     var line = new THREE.Line(geometry, material);
     scene.add(line);

     renderer.render(scene, camera);

     // remove the line from scene, just to be clean
     scene.remove(scene.objects[0]);

   }
```


